(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-lung-thorax-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Lung & Thorax Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Regarding lobar anatomy, the right lung is divided into upper, middle, and lower lobes, while the left lung has only...","choices":["upper, middle, and lower lobes, identical to the right lung's three lobes","upper and lower lobes (with the lingular segment of the upper lobe)","a single undivided lobe, with no further lobar division present at all","four separate lobes, one more than the right lung's three lobes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unlike the right lung's three lobes, the left lung has only upper and lower lobes; the lingular segment of the left upper lobe is analogous in position to the right middle lobe. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The minor (horizontal) fissure specifically divides which lobes?","choices":["the right middle and lower lobes","the left upper and lower lobes","there is no minor fissure in normal anatomy","the right upper and middle lobes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The minor (horizontal) fissure divides the right upper lobe from the right middle lobe; the major (oblique) fissures separate the upper/middle lobes from the lower lobes on each side. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Comparing the right and left main-stem bronchi, the right main-stem bronchus is characteristically...","choices":["identical in length, width, and angle to the left bronchus","shorter, wider, and more straight (vertical) than the left","longer, narrower, and more angled (horizontal) than the right","absent entirely, with only a single shared main bronchus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right main-stem bronchus is shorter, wider, and takes a more straight/vertical course compared with the left, which is why aspirated foreign material more often lodges in the right lung. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The social history relevant to a pulmonary evaluation includes employment, home environment, tobacco use, exposure to respiratory infection (for example, TB (tuberculosis)), nutrition, travel, hobbies, and...","choices":["insurance status and payer type","the patient's ABO blood type and Rh factor","nothing beyond tobacco use history","alcohol/drug use and exercise capacity"],"answer":3,"exp":"The personal/social history checklist for a pulmonary evaluation includes employment, home environment, tobacco use, TB (tuberculosis)/respiratory infection exposure, nutrition, travel, hobbies, and alcohol/drug use along with exercise capacity. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Equipment needed for a thorough thoracic/pulmonary physical exam includes a stethoscope, marking pencil, appropriate drapes, and...","choices":["an otoscope","a blood pressure cuff exclusively","no equipment is needed for this exam","a centimeter ruler/tape measure"],"answer":3,"exp":"A centimeter ruler or tape measure is needed to measure findings such as diaphragmatic excursion distance, alongside the stethoscope, marking pencil, and appropriate draping. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"For proper auscultation of the lungs, the recommended patient positioning includes the head bent forward and...","choices":["no particular positioning is needed","arms raised straight overhead","lying flat on the back with arms at the sides","arms folded over the anterior chest"],"answer":3,"exp":"Recommended positioning for lung auscultation has the patient's head bent forward with arms folded over the anterior chest, which helps spread the scapulae and improve access to the posterior lung fields. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"During lung auscultation, which part of the stethoscope is used, and how should the patient breathe?","choices":["the diaphragm; the patient breathes rapidly through the nose","no stethoscope is needed for this exam","the diaphragm; the patient breathes slowly through the mouth","the bell; the patient holds their breath throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lung auscultation uses the diaphragm of the stethoscope, with the patient breathing slowly through an open mouth (rather than the nose), which produces more reliable, comparable breath sounds. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"When percussing the posterior chest, the examiner should proceed through the intercostal spaces, comparing side to side, and also assess the...","choices":["only the abdomen, entirely skipping the chest during this particular portion of the exam","only the posterior chest, with absolutely no other chest regions assessed at any point","axillary region and anterior chest, in addition to the posterior chest and diaphragmatic excursion","only the cardiac apex region, using the bell of the stethoscope in that one spot exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"A complete percussion exam covers the posterior chest (intercostal spaces, superior to inferior), the axillary region, the anterior chest, and diaphragmatic excursion, not the posterior chest alone. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"Pectus carinatum, another chest wall deformity screened for, involves...","choices":["outward protrusion/bulging of the sternum","inward depression of the sternal body","a completely flat, unremarkable sternum","a finding detected only by chest percussion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pectus carinatum (sometimes called 'pigeon chest') involves the sternum protruding outward, in contrast to pectus excavatum, where the sternum is depressed inward. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 31"},{"q":"Generalized skin inspection during the thoracic exam specifically screens for...","choices":["hearing acuity","blood pressure","bowel sounds","cyanosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Generalized skin inspection during the thoracic/pulmonary exam specifically screens for cyanosis (a bluish discoloration suggesting inadequate oxygenation), alongside checking nail beds, breath odor, and respiratory rate/pattern. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Along with cyanosis screening, thoracic inspection also includes assessment of the nail beds, breath odor, and...","choices":["the patient's measured liver span","the patient's visual acuity","respiratory rate and pattern","the character of bowel sounds"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inspection checklist for the thorax includes generalized skin assessment (cyanosis), nail beds, breath odor, and respiratory rate and pattern, providing an overall picture before moving to palpation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"The muscles of respiration include the diaphragm (which moves downward during inspiration to increase intrathoracic space), the intercostals, and which accessory muscles?","choices":["SCM (sternocleidomastoid) and trapezius","biceps and triceps","quadriceps and hamstrings","no accessory muscles are used in breathing"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles serve as accessory muscles of respiration, becoming more prominently active during labored or increased-effort breathing. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"During inspiration, the diaphragm's downward movement serves to...","choices":["decrease intrathoracic space, raising intrathoracic pressure and pushing air out","increase intrathoracic space, lowering intrathoracic pressure and drawing air in","move further upward, not downward, throughout the entire phase of inspiration","have essentially no measurable effect on thoracic volume during breathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"As the diaphragm contracts and moves downward, it increases the volume (space) of the thoracic cavity, which lowers intrathoracic pressure below atmospheric pressure and draws air into the lungs. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Among the anatomic landmarks used to describe chest findings are the midclavicular line and the anterior, mid, and posterior...","choices":["axillary lines","inguinal lines","there are no standardized landmark lines for the chest","popliteal lines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Standard thoracic landmark lines include the midclavicular line as well as the anterior, mid, and posterior axillary lines, plus the mid-thoracic and mid-scapular lines, used to precisely localize findings. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"A humorous but practical suggestion in lecture for the examiner performing diaphragmatic excursion assessment is to...","choices":["never breathe at all yourself during the entire remainder of the comprehensive physical examination visit","hold your own breath while assessing the patient, since if you run out of air, a patient with respiratory compromise likely will too","this particular lecture tip has no real practical clinical value whatsoever in everyday practice","ask the patient to hold the examiner's breath for them throughout the entire diaphragmatic excursion maneuver"],"answer":1,"exp":"This practical tip suggests the examiner hold their own breath during the maneuver — if the examiner (with presumably normal lung function) runs out of air, a patient with a respiratory problem is even more likely to be struggling, providing an intuitive sense of the patient's respiratory reserve. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"The comprehensive chest exam summary organizes assessment into inspection (size/shape/symmetry/color/venous patterns/rib prominence/respiratory rate/accessory muscles), palpation (tenderness/expansion/pulsations/crepitus/fremitus), percussion (tone/intensity/pitch/duration/quality/excursion), and auscultation, which itself includes intensity, pitch, duration, quality, adventitious findings, and...","choices":["the patient's blood pressure and pulse rate","the character of any bowel sounds heard","vocal resonance (bronchophony, egophony)","the patient's measured visual acuity score"],"answer":2,"exp":"The auscultation summary specifically includes assessing breath sound intensity, pitch, duration, and quality, screening for adventitious sounds, and assessing vocal resonance findings such as bronchophony and egophony. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"The characteristics assessed for each percussion note (whether over the chest or elsewhere) include tone, intensity, pitch, duration, and...","choices":["quality","visual acuity","blood type","heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"A thorough percussion assessment characterizes the note's tone, intensity, pitch, duration, and quality, together helping distinguish resonant, dull, tympanic, or other percussion notes. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 62"},{"q":"The visceral and parietal pleura are the two pleural layers lining the right and left pleural cavities; the visceral pleura specifically...","choices":["is absent in normal anatomy","is identical in location to the parietal pleura","lines the inner chest wall, not the lung","directly covers the lung surface itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"The visceral pleura is the layer directly covering the surface of the lung, while the parietal pleura lines the inner surface of the chest wall; the potential space between them is the pleural space. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The lungs receive their nutrient blood supply from the bronchial arteries (branching from the thoracic aorta/intercostal arteries), with blood returning via the...","choices":["hepatic veins","bronchial veins","pulmonary veins exclusively","renal veins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nutrient blood supply to the lung tissue itself (distinct from the pulmonary circulation for gas exchange) comes via bronchial arteries and returns via bronchial veins. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Anatomically, the right lung's oblique fissure is marked by the 5th rib laterally and which rib at the midclavicular line medially?","choices":["there is no fissure on the right lung","the 6th rib","the 2nd rib","the 12th rib"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right lung's oblique fissure surface projection runs from the 5th rib laterally to the 6th rib at the midclavicular line medially, separating the lower lobe from the upper/middle lobes. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"On posterior surface projection, T10 marks the inferior border of the lungs on expiration, extending down to approximately...","choices":["L5, well into the lumbar spine","it remains fixed at T10 regardless of breathing phase","C7, well above the thoracic landmarks","T12 on full inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"On the posterior chest, the inferior lung border is at approximately T10 during expiration and extends down to about T12 with full inspiration, reflecting the normal excursion of the lung bases with breathing. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Vocal resonance techniques (bronchophony, egophony, whispered pectoriloquy) are all methods of assessing...","choices":["the patient's blood pressure indirectly through vessel sounds heard","the quality and character of the patient's bowel sounds","how well the underlying lung tissue transmits the spoken/whispered voice","visible chest wall movement during quiet, resting breathing"],"answer":2,"exp":"All three vocal resonance techniques assess how effectively the underlying lung tissue transmits vocal sound to the chest wall/stethoscope, which changes characteristically with consolidation, effusion, or pneumothorax. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"Family history relevant to a pulmonary evaluation specifically focuses on chronic conditions, especially...","choices":["family history is not relevant to this exam","only dermatologic conditions","pulmonary and cardiac conditions","only orthopedic conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"The family history component of a pulmonary evaluation specifically emphasizes chronic conditions, especially pulmonary and cardiac disease, given their relevance to the patient's own respiratory risk. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Past medical history relevant to the pulmonary exam should include prior trauma and chronic conditions, especially...","choices":["past medical history is not relevant to this exam","only dental history","only prior eye surgeries","pulmonary and cardiac conditions"],"answer":3,"exp":"The past medical history component emphasizes prior trauma and chronic conditions, especially pulmonary and cardiac disease, alongside relevant prior testing such as skin tests, CXR (chest x-ray), and PFTs. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Systematic percussion of the posterior chest, moving superior to inferior and comparing side to side, helps the examiner detect asymmetric findings because...","choices":["percussion is fundamentally incapable of ever detecting asymmetry between the two sides","percussion notes are always identical between sides, regardless of underlying pathology","comparing corresponding areas on each side makes subtle unilateral differences easier to notice","only the left side needs to be percussed, since the right side is rarely clinically affected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Directly comparing percussion notes at corresponding levels on the left and right sides makes it much easier to detect a subtle unilateral abnormality (for example, localized dullness suggesting effusion or consolidation) than assessing each side in isolation. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"The comprehensive chest exam summary for inspection also includes assessment of color, superficial venous patterns, and...","choices":["the character of any audible bowel sounds","prominence of ribs and respiratory rate/rhythm","the patient's percussed liver span measurement","the patient's fingerstick blood glucose level"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inspection summary includes size/shape, symmetry, color, superficial venous patterns, prominence of ribs, respiratory rate/rhythm, and accessory muscle use. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"The comprehensive chest exam summary for palpation includes tenderness, thoracic expansion, pulsations, tactile fremitus, and...","choices":["crepitus, grating, and vibrations","bowel sound character alone","blood pressure readings only","visual acuity readings only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The palpation summary specifically includes assessing for crepitus, grating sensations, and vibrations, alongside tenderness, thoracic expansion, pulsations, and tactile fremitus. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 61"},{"q":"A systematic auscultation approach for the lungs involves comparing side to side while the patient breathes slowly through the mouth, which helps the examiner detect...","choices":["only findings present on the left side of the chest, never the right","asymmetric or adventitious findings that might be missed without direct comparison","nothing useful, since breath sounds are always identical regardless of pathology","only heart sounds, not any lung sounds, using the stethoscope diaphragm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systematically comparing breath sounds at corresponding points on each side, with controlled slow mouth-breathing, makes it easier to detect subtle asymmetries or adventitious sounds (crackles, wheezes, and so on) that might otherwise be missed. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"Symmetric chest expansion, assessed by watching thumb separation during a deep breath, being roughly equal on both sides is a normal finding because it reflects...","choices":["complete lung collapse occurring on one side only","it only applies to assessment of the upper lobes","a finding with no clinical significance at all","equal, unobstructed inflation of both lungs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Symmetric chest expansion reflects that both lungs are inflating roughly equally and without significant obstruction or restriction on either side; asymmetric expansion (reduced on one side) suggests a unilateral process such as pneumothorax, effusion, or consolidation limiting that side's expansion. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Considered together, the four-part chest exam (inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation) summary findings — symmetric/normal in each category — provide a baseline for detecting...","choices":["findings entirely unrelated to any organ system in the body at all","deviations from normal that suggest underlying pulmonary or cardiac pathology","only the cosmetic, external appearance of the chest wall itself","nothing useful, since normal findings are never clinically relevant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Knowing what normal findings look like across each of the four exam components (inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation) gives the examiner a baseline for recognizing meaningful deviations that suggest underlying pulmonary or cardiac pathology. — The Physical Exam of the Lungs lecture, Slide 58"}]};
